On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 02:44:40 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> - How does it work when a thread has multiple levels to it? Say there's
>   a first message, and then two separate replies to it. Then replies
>   each of those replies - so essentially two branches. Does adding this
>   header kill the entire thread, or just the branch?

As I described it, the whole thread, since notmuch tags by thread. But
your question shows that's the wrong scope. It works better per
message. A close header would only mean "this message needs no reply".
Its parent is already answered by the closing message itself, so
nothing else needs to change. Each branch then ends on its own, and a
sibling branch stays open until someone ends it.

At that scope, the header and your archive dir do the same job from
opposite ends. The header lets the sender mark a message as needing no
reply. The archive lets the recipient decide it needs none. More on
that below.

> - Is it possible to just add a header to an existing message? The files
>   are in version control, so we could modify the files instead of
>   posting a new message. Essentially we're adding metadata to existing
>   files.

It's possible, but I'd avoid it, for three reasons:

- The archive would no longer match what was sent. Every message is
  byte-for-byte what its author delivered, and a patch's headers are
  what git am reads. Editing them in place turns the list from a
  record into mutable state.

- Two people editing the same file's headers conflict in jj. A file
  only gets added or moved, and that merges cleanly.

- notmuch won't see the edit. I tried it on a scratch copy of the
  list: after adding a header to a file in cur/, notmuch new reported
  "No new mail" and the header was not searchable until notmuch
  reindex. notmuch tracks files by name, not content. mail-tag.sh
  could read the files directly, as it does for In-Reply-To, but
  that's working around notmuch rather than with it.

Moving a file keeps the metadata in the repo without touching the
message. That's your archive idea.

> No I don't think we need this, at least not right now. I'd like to try
> to keep things as simple as possible and derive the list state from the
> mail branch.

Agreed, dropping it.

> Another idea I had was an archive dir, and so mute a thread by moving
> messages into the archive dir. This can be done on a per-message basis,
> and doesn't change any of the existing rules. i.e. if claude sends the
> last message, and I archive it, it will no longer show up in my inbox
> but claude doesn't see it as something as needing to be
> responded. Archived messages would add the archived tag and remove inbox
> tag.

I like this better than the header, and I'd go with it alone. It
covers both of your cases with no new message. It works per message,
so it answers the branch question directly. It doesn't touch the
existing rules. It's derived from the mail branch, since the path is
part of the commit. And it covers my side too: when you reply
"applied, thanks", I archive that message on my bookmark and push it,
instead of answering with a reply that lands in your inbox.

I checked the mechanics on the scratch copy. With maildir/archive/
{cur,new,tmp}, moving a file there makes notmuch new report "Detected 1
file rename". The message keeps its id and thread, and
path:archive/** matches it. In mail-tag.sh it's one more line after
the replied ones:

    +archived -inbox -- path:archive/**

One thing to decide: the archive is shared. With just the two of us
that doesn't matter, because a message is only ever in one inbox.
Yours holds my mail and mine holds yours. With a third participant, a
message from you to the list sits in both our inboxes, and whoever
archives it archives it for both. Per-user dirs (archive/pat/,
archive/claude/) don't fix that, because a file can only be in one of
them. Per-user state would need something like a per-user list of
archived Message-IDs. I'd start with one shared archive/ and only
revisit if a third person joins.

If that works for you, I'll send a series that:

  - adds the archived tag to dotfiles/notmuch/hooks/mail-tag.sh,
  - updates "Indexing mail" in zib/patch-review-workflow.org, and
  - changes the process-email skill so a message that needs no answer
    is archived rather than replied to. The skill currently insists
    every inbox message gets a reply, which is what keeps these
    threads alive.
